---
import OdeV2Layout from '../../../layouts/OdeV2Layout.astro';
import Section from '../../../components/ode/Section.astro';
import DataTag from '../../../components/ode-v2/DataTag.astro';
import Status from '../../../components/ode-v2/Status.astro';
import Finding from '../../../components/ode-v2/Finding.astro';
import Callout from '../../../components/ode-v2/Callout.astro';
import StatCard from '../../../components/ode-v2/StatCard.astro';
import ReadNext from '../../../components/ode-v2/ReadNext.astro';
import { odeMeta, odeRoutes } from '../../../i18n/ode-v2';

const lang = 'en' as const;
const m = odeMeta[lang].tesi;
const r = odeRoutes[lang];

const acid = [
  { m: 'Crude bleached calf tallow', v: '0.8', ffa: '0.4%', n: 'The raw material starts out well on the one parameter the chemistry says matters.' },
  { m: 'Pure rendered lard, reference limit', v: '1.3', ffa: '0.65%', n: 'The tightest limit in the whole animal fat family. It says something about how the raw material is selected, not about the species.' },
  { m: 'Edible tallow, reference limit', v: '2.5', ffa: '1.25%', n: 'A food-grade trading threshold, not a skin safety one. Nobody has ever tied it to anything that happens on skin.' },
  { m: 'Chemically interesterified tallow, 9:1 blend', v: '17.4', ffa: '8.8%', n: 'About seven times the reference limit. This is the material the thin human evidence actually sits on.' },
];

const profile = [
  { fa: 'C16:0 palmitic', a: '29.88%', b: '30.3%' },
  { fa: 'C18:0 stearic', a: '12.00%', b: '16.2%' },
  { fa: 'C18:1 oleic', a: '45.19%', b: '35.4%' },
  { fa: 'C14:0 myristic', a: '5.76%', b: '6.3%' },
  { fa: 'C16:1 palmitoleic', a: '5.38%', b: '3.2%' },
  { fa: 'C18:2 linoleic', a: '0.50%', b: '1.3%' },
];

const lipase = [
  { mat: 'Beef tallow, pork fat', cls: 'Triglycerides', bond: 'Yes, three ester bonds per molecule', out: 'Exposed' },
  { mat: 'Vegetable oils (coconut, olive, sunflower)', cls: 'Triglycerides', bond: 'Yes', out: 'Exposed as much as tallow' },
  { mat: 'Petrolatum', cls: 'Mixture of saturated hydrocarbons', bond: 'No', out: 'Immune' },
  { mat: 'Mineral oil', cls: 'Liquid hydrocarbons', bond: 'No', out: 'Immune' },
  { mat: 'Squalane', cls: 'Hydrocarbon', bond: 'No', out: 'Immune' },
  { mat: 'Dimethicone and silicones', cls: 'Siloxane polymer', bond: 'No', out: 'Immune' },
];

const cannot = [
  {
    t: 'Occlusion, against petrolatum',
    d: 'No triglyceride gets near petrolatum as an occlusive. We do not claim we beat it, and we will not start claiming it if the experiment goes our way either. The best we could hope for is non-inferiority to mineral oil.',
  },
  {
    t: 'Barrier repair, against ceramide systems',
    d: 'In a randomised trial on 121 patients, severity scores for a physiological lipid mixture in the right ratio came out no different from a mid-potency corticosteroid at 28 days. No significant difference is not the same as proven equivalence. Tallow supplies neither ceramides nor cholesterol in physiological ratio, so we make no barrier repair claim in that sense.',
  },
  {
    t: 'Stickiness, on the primary endpoint of our own category',
    d: 'Tallow sits in the emollient category, whose whole job is to give the surface back its softness, flexibility and slip. Stickiness delivers the opposite friction profile. It was measured on a texture analyser, on beef tallow: an instrument, on our own matrix, not a sensory panel on somebody else’s. That is a failure on the category’s main endpoint, not a footnote about acceptability.',
  },
  {
    t: 'Linoleic acid content',
    d: 'Tallow is poor in linoleic acid: between 0.5% and 1.3% across two independent characterisations. The cosmetic lipid literature hands most of the barrier credit to oils rich in linoleic and alpha-linolenic acid. That is bad news for us, and it comes with no softening clause.',
  },
];
---
<OdeV2Layout title={m.title} description={m.description} lang={lang} active="tesi">
  <header class="pg">
    <p class="ode-eyebrow">The Thesis</p>
    <h1>One parameter decides, and almost nobody declares it</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Here is the chemistry OdE rests on, the complication that eats into it, and why both point
      to the same conclusion: the product is the qualification, not the tallow. Our strongest
      argument and our worst vulnerability, in the order a competent technical reader would run
      into them.
    </p>
  </header>

  <Section n="1" title="The principle is about one molecule, not a class">
    <p>
      A fatty acid locked into a triglyceride behaves like a brick in a wall. Set
      <strong>oleic acid</strong> loose and it behaves like a brick that has come out: it slips
      between the ordered layers of the skin's own barrier and pushes them apart.
    </p>
    <p>
      Somebody has measured this directly. On the human forearm, with the substances applied neat
      and no penetration enhancer in the vehicle, free oleic acid and blends carrying more than
      25% of it in free form significantly raised transepidermal water loss and tracer
      penetration at 24 hours. The corresponding triglyceride at 100% moved neither.
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Mack Correa MC, Mao G, Saad P, Flach CR, Mendelsohn R, Walters RM. Molecular interactions of plant oil components with stratum corneum lipids. Exp Dermatol 2014;23(1):39-44. DOI 10.1111/exd.12296. Human volar forearm in vivo, neat substances, no propylene glycol." />
    </p>
    <p class="ev-line"><Status kind="dimostrato" lang={lang} /> <Finding kind="pro" lang={lang} /></p>

    <Callout tone="warn" title="A correction that concerns us, and that runs opposite to what we expected">
      <p>
        Until the latest revision of our research we stated this principle in general terms: a
        free fatty acid disturbs the barrier, the esterified form does not.
        <strong>That was wrong, through imprecision.</strong> The study tests oleic acid against
        its own triglyceride. The result is about oleic acid, not about free fatty acids as a
        class, and it does not carry over to the saturated ones.
      </p>
      <p>
        The distinction matters, because the two dominant saturated fatty acids in tallow,
        palmitic and stearic, come to roughly 46% of the profile between them, and the evidence
        on those runs the other way. Applied to human skin ex vivo, they increase lipid
        production and transport in the stratum corneum.
        <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Nip J et al., Unilever R&amp;D. International Journal of Cosmetic Science 2024. DOI 10.1111/ics.12961. Human skin ex vivo, tape-stripped, isolated fatty acids rather than tallow. Grade B1, evidence translated from damaged skin to the maintenance of healthy skin; authors with a declarable commercial interest." />
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>What this does NOT let us say.</strong> Not that tallow strengthens the barrier,
        and not that it repairs it. The study is ex vivo, on damaged skin, with isolated fatty
        acids rather than tallow, and its authors have a commercial interest. There is exactly
        one thing it is good for: <strong>it kills the assumption that everything hydrolysis
        releases must be harmful</strong>. That is the premise of section 3, and nothing beyond
        it.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <p>
      In tallow, the fatty acid is <strong>bound</strong>. So the question is not whether tallow
      contains oleic acid. It is <strong>how much of it has come loose</strong>. That number has
      a name, a standard and a price. It is the acid value.
    </p>

    <Callout tone="honest" title="The two qualifications a technical reader will check first">
      <p>
        <strong>Nobody has shown it transfers.</strong> Almost all the barrier evidence uses
        liquid unsaturated vegetable oils, not semisolid saturated animal fats. The principle is
        mechanistically sound and fits the corpus, but no one has run that comparison on a
        saturated animal fat.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>No acid value threshold has ever been validated against a skin outcome.</strong>
        Not for tallow, not for any other topical lipid. The limits below are trading grades,
        written for another purpose entirely. Pinning down how acid value relates to barrier
        outcome is the research space we are claiming, and
        <a href={r.evidenza}>nobody has walked it yet</a>.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Section>

  <Section n="2" title="Acid value, and what it costs to know it">
    <p>
      Acid value counts how many fatty acids have come off the triglyceride, in milligrams of
      potassium hydroxide per gram of fat. It is a standardised parameter, the determination is a
      titration, and an accredited laboratory will do it for a few tens of euros off a published
      price list.
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Acid value and free fatty acids according to EN ISO 660. Indicative price of roughly 35-60 euros per sample on the public price list of an analytical laboratory, verified in research track 6 v2 section 8 and in the OdE research consolidation, part D.3." />
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      Measurable, cheap, and the parameter that section 1 says safety turns on. And almost nobody
      in this market puts it on the sheet.
    </p>

    <div class="tbl-wrap">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Material or limit</th><th scope="col">mg KOH/g</th><th scope="col">FFA</th><th scope="col">What it means</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {acid.map((a) => (
            <tr><th scope="row">{a.m}</th><td class="num">{a.v}</td><td class="num">{a.ffa}</td><td>{a.n}</td></tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Values measured on crude calf tallow and on interesterified blends: Kowalska M, Woźniak M, Żbikowska A, Kozłowska M. Biomolecules 2020;10:115. DOI 10.3390/biom10010115, determination per ISO 660, mean of three determinations." />
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Acid value and peroxide value limits for pure rendered lard, rendered pork fat, premier jus and edible tallow: FAO/WHO Codex Alimentarius, Standard for Named Animal Fats CXS 211-1999, Section 3 and Appendix, primary text." />
    </p>

    <h3 class="sub-h">The process generates acidity, and the process is controllable</h3>
    <p class="measure">
      Run two rendering methods on the same raw material and the process conditions decide the
      outcome. Rendering cold rather than hot gives an acid value roughly 3.8 times lower and
      peroxides roughly 2.3 times lower, and the differences are statistically significant.
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Limmatvapirat C, Limmatvapirat S, Krongrawa W, et al. Beef tallow: extraction, physicochemical property, fatty acid composition, antioxidant activity, and formulation of lotion bars. J Appl Pharm Sci 2021;11(09):018-028. DOI 10.7324/JAPS.2021.110903. Acid value 0.20-0.22 with rendering at 80 °C for 10 minutes, against 0.73-0.76 with microwave rendering at 135 °C for 5 minutes." />
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      The same study has to be read straight where it does not suit us, too: the differences in
      antioxidant activity between methods are <strong>not</strong> significant. Rendering method
      moves acidity and peroxides. It does not move antioxidant activity.
    </p>
    <Callout tone="honest" title="And a tension with no single answer">
      <p>
        The same work shows the softer fraction of tallow carries more unsaturates and measures
        higher for antioxidant activity. But more unsaturates also means
        <strong>it oxidises more readily over time</strong> and it means a
        <strong>lower solid fat content at skin temperature</strong>, which is less of the very
        physical class section 4 rests on.
      </p>
      <p>
        The fraction that wins on in vitro antioxidant activity is not the one that wins on shelf
        life, and neither is the one that wins on staying put on skin.
        <strong>There is no single best fraction. There is a trade-off curve, and somebody has to
        map it rather than assert it.</strong> That is one of the experiments in
        <a href={r.evidenza}>Open questions</a>, and one of the dearest.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <p class="ev-line"><Status kind="dimostrato" lang={lang} /> <Finding kind="pro" lang={lang} /></p>

    <Callout tone="warn" title="A detail that reverses how the existing evidence reads">
      <p>
        The only studies with a human outcome on tallow use chemically interesterified material,
        with an acid value up to about seven times the reference limit. That is exactly the kind
        of material section 1 flags as potentially disruptive.
      </p>
      <p>
        This does not work in our favour. It means
        <strong>the literature is describing a different material from ours</strong>: ovine,
        interesterified, blended, high in acidity. Which means we cannot use it for or against
        anything.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h3 class="sub-h">The profile, for completeness</h3>
    <div class="tbl-wrap">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Fatty acid</th><th scope="col">Characterisation A</th><th scope="col">Characterisation B</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {profile.map((p) => (
            <tr class:list={[p.fa.includes('linoleic') && 'is-gap']}>
              <th scope="row">{p.fa}</th><td class="num">{p.a}</td><td class="num">{p.b}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      Two independent gas chromatography characterisations with flame ionisation detection, both
      on beef tallow.
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Characterisation A: Limmatvapirat 2021, DOI 10.7324/JAPS.2021.110903, low-temperature sample. Characterisation B: Kowalska 2020, DOI 10.3390/biom10010115, crude calf tallow. Highlighted row: linoleic acid content, an unfavourable datum." />
      The dashed row is the one that counts against us, and it is dashed on purpose.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section n="3" title="The complication, stated where the claim is expected">
    <p>
      The bacteria living on skin secrete lipases, enzymes whose whole job is cutting exactly
      that bond. They can free the fatty acid <strong>where the product is sitting</strong>, long
      after it has left our hands. Tallow that goes onto skin with a low acid value does not
      necessarily stay that way.
    </p>
    <p class="ev-line"><Status kind="dimostrato" lang={lang} /> <Finding kind="contro" lang={lang} /></p>
    <p class="measure">
      The mechanism is certain. <strong>Which way it pushes is not</strong>, and that is a recent
      revision of our research we have to state in full, because it changes what kind of problem
      this is. Lipases do not release one substance. They release a <strong>mixture</strong>:
      roughly 38% oleic acid, which section 1 says counts against us, and roughly 46% palmitic
      and stearic, where the evidence runs the other way.
      <strong>Nobody has ever measured the net effect.</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      That is not good news dressed up as bad. It moves us from a standoff between sources to a
      defined experimental gap, and a gap is a better place to be only because you can close it.
      The practical consequence is exact, and it sits in
      <a href={r.evidenza}>Open questions</a>: the experiment must not measure how fast the
      material hydrolyses. It must measure <strong>what</strong> comes off.
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      Every link in the chain is documented: skin lipases have been characterised, the
      triglycerides of the fatty acids in tallow are their substrates, and hand an intact
      triglyceride to a skin bacterium and it gets cleaved, releasing the fatty acid.
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Ingham E, Holland KT, Gowland G, Cunliffe WJ. Partial purification and characterization of lipase from Propionibacterium acnes. J Gen Microbiol 1981;124:393-401. DOI 10.1099/00221287-124-2-393." />
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Bomar L, Brugger SD, Yost BH, Davies SS, Lemon KP. Corynebacterium accolens releases antipneumococcal free fatty acids from human nostril and skin surface triacylglycerols. mBio 2016;7:e01725-15. DOI 10.1128/mBio.01725-15. Documented hydrolysis of intact triolein." />
    </p>

    <h3 class="sub-h">How large the effect is, with the data we have</h3>
    <p class="measure">
      The number people quote, roughly 90% hydrolysis, was measured <strong>inside the
      comedo</strong>: a sealed space, dense with bacteria, poorly oxygenated, with a long
      residence time. The same study, on the same population, measures roughly 25% on the
      <strong>surface of intact skin</strong>. And there is a measured ceiling on what bacteria
      contribute: wipe out 99% of them and surface free fatty acids fall by only half.
    </p>
    <div class="grid">
      <StatCard value="about 25%" caption="Hydrolysed fraction on the surface of intact skin, in the same study that measures roughly 90% inside the comedo." kind="fonte" source="Nicolaides N, Wells GC, et al. Lipid composition of comedones compared with that of human skin surface. J Invest Dermatol 1970;54:487-495. PMID 5454099." />
      <StatCard value="at most half" caption="Share of the surface free fatty acid load attributable to bacteria: eliminating 99% of them reduces it by only 50%." kind="fonte" source="Marples RR, Downing DT, Kligman AM. Control of free fatty acids in human surface lipids by Corynebacterium acnes. J Invest Dermatol 1971;56:127-131. PMID 4997367." />
      <StatCard value="not measured" caption="The composition of the mixture released from OdE material, which is the datum that decides the sign of the effect. No study has measured which fatty acids appear, and in what proportion, after applying an intact triglyceride to human skin." kind="bench" />
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <Status kind="costruzione" lang={lang} />
      Reading those two facts as putting the expected order of magnitude on intact skin nearer
      25% than 90% is <strong>an inference from anatomy</strong>, not a measurement on tallow. We
      label it that way, and we will not use it any other way.
    </p>

    <h3 class="sub-h">The asymmetry, which is the worst part</h3>
    <p class="measure">
      A lipase hydrolyses the carboxylic ester bond, so for it to do anything, that bond has to be
      there. Sort the materials tallow gets compared with on that one test and they fall into two
      clean groups.
    </p>
    <div class="tbl-wrap">
      <table class="tbl">
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Material</th><th scope="col">Chemical class</th><th scope="col">Ester bonds</th><th scope="col">Outcome</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {lipase.map((l) => (
            <tr class:list={[l.out === 'Exposed' && 'is-gap']}>
              <th scope="row">{l.mat}</th><td>{l.cls}</td><td>{l.bond}</td><td>{l.out}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Classification by chemical class, and verification of alternative degradation routes for hydrocarbons and silicones, which are documented only in environmental matrices and not in skin commensals. Research track 6 v2, section 5.11.1." />
    </p>

    <Callout tone="honest" title="What this means exactly, with nothing softened">
      <p>
        This is not a matter of degree. It is a <strong>difference of chemical class</strong>.
        No process condition, no control of incoming acid value and no purification protocol will
        make a triglyceride immune to an enzyme that cuts ester bonds. The vulnerability is
        structural, and our commercially relevant comparators do not share it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Three qualifications draw the boundary, and you need all three to avoid both overstating
        and understating it. One: <strong>vegetable oils are just as exposed</strong>, so on the
        ground of tallow against vegetable oils there is no asymmetry at all. It exists only
        against the hydrocarbon and silicone block. Two:
        <strong>their immunity is not a benefit, it is the absence of a mechanism</strong>.
        Petrolatum and dimethicone do not generate free fatty acids, but then they do not generate
        anything. They are inert. Three: <strong>the asymmetry is about whether the mechanism
        exists, not about which way it cuts</strong>. That we are exposed and they are not is a
        fact. That the exposure does harm is exactly what nobody has measured.
      </p>
    </Callout>

    <h3 class="sub-h">How we phrase the claim, in full</h3>
    <blockquote class="claim">
      <p>
        Our tallow reaches the skin carrying almost no free fatty acids, in an oxidative state we
        control and certify batch by batch. Like any lipid with ester bonds in it, which means
        like every vegetable oil it gets compared with, it is a substrate for microbial skin
        lipases, and some of it hydrolyses in place. What data we have on intact skin points to
        that happening far less than it does inside a closed follicle. What comes off is a
        mixture, and the evidence on its two main components runs in opposite directions: against
        us for oleic acid, for us on palmitic and stearic.
        <strong>Nobody has measured the net effect on our material, and we do not claim to know
        it.</strong> We are designing the experiment that would measure what comes off, not just
        how fast. Against hydrocarbon and silicone comparators, which have no ester bonds and are
        inert to this mechanism, we are exposed and they are not. Our thesis is not that we match
        them for inertness. It is that what we release is physiologically compatible and
        quantitatively contained, and that is what we intend to show.
      </p>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="note">
      It is longer than an advertising line and it sells worse. It is also the only version that
      survives a competent formulator, which is what the <a href={r.audit}>Audit</a> is for.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section n="4" title="Physical state: the one genuinely favourable element, with its limits">
    <p>
      At the skin surface, around 32 °C, tallow is semisolid, with an estimated solid fat content
      of roughly 21%. That puts it in the physical class of petrolatum, whose melting range
      starts above skin temperature, rather than with the liquid oils.
      <DataTag kind="stima" label="Solid fat content at 32 °C obtained by linear interpolation between 24.0% at 30 °C and 17.3% at 35 °C, pNMR measurements per AOCS Cd 16b-93. [DERIVED CALCULATION], not a direct measurement. The estimate is plausible but at the high end of the expected range, which is 15-25% at 32 °C: no publication reports an explicit numerical value for native tallow at that temperature, only curves in figures. Research track 4 v2 section 2.1, qualified by the multilingual library." />
    </p>
    <p class="measure">
      On this axis we also have a measured, citable number rather than an estimate: tallow is
      <strong>fully melted only at 46 °C</strong>, while cocoa butter has gone liquid by 35 to
      37 °C. At skin temperature the order runs palm stearin, then beef tallow, then cocoa
      butter.
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Osborn HT, Akoh CC. Journal of Food Science 2002. DOI 10.1111/j.1365-2621.2002.tb08762.x. Comparison of melting profiles. It is the primary datum anchoring the thermal positioning of the material." />
    </p>
    <p class="ev-line"><Status kind="costruzione" lang={lang} /> <Finding kind="pro" lang={lang} /></p>
    <p class="measure">
      It matters because the most hostile prediction in the literature, that a high-oleic
      triglyceride will not reduce transepidermal water loss, was established on a
      <strong>liquid</strong> vegetable oil. If physical state is the variable separating the two
      arms of that comparison, the prediction does not automatically carry over to tallow.
    </p>

    <Callout tone="honest" title="What this argument does NOT demonstrate">
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>It does not show that tallow occludes.</strong> Not one measurement of
          transepidermal water loss on pure tallow applied as it comes turned up in any of the
          research tracks.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Petrolatum does not work by film alone.</strong> It gets into the stratum
          corneum and interacts with the intercellular lipids. If much of what it does depends on
          that interaction, then sharing a physical state buys us nothing.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The two are semisolid for different reasons.</strong> By 32 °C tallow has
          already shed roughly four fifths of the solid fraction it had at room temperature. We
          found no solid fat content data for petrolatum by a comparable method, so the
          comparison stays qualitative.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The 79% that is liquid is wide open.</strong> Even if the semisolid state did
          slow hydrolysis, the protection would cover about a fifth of the material, and slowing
          something down is not stopping it.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Which is why the threshold goes down <strong>now, before the measurement</strong>: if
        solid fat content at 32 °C comes back under 15%, the petrolatum-class argument is dead
        and the material moves in with the semi-liquid oils. The measurement costs a few hundred
        euros, and it is the first analytical spend in the plan rather than the last.
      </p>
    </Callout>
    <p class="note">
      That the semisolid state slows hydrolysis <strong>is a hypothesis</strong>. It is built by
      analogy with digestive lipases in simulated digestion models, not with skin lipases. Until
      somebody tests it, it does not enter the positioning as a protective factor, and the
      criterion that would kill it is already written down in
      <a href={r.evidenza}>Open questions</a>.
      <DataTag kind="bench" label="Analogy with Hart K, Lin S, Thilakarathna SH, Wright AJ. Emulsion droplet crystallinity attenuates early in vitro digestive lipolysis. Food Chem 2018;260:145-151. DOI 10.1016/j.foodchem.2018.03.142. The same study shows the difference vanishes by the end of digestion. No data on microbial skin lipases exists." />
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section n="5" title="Where we cannot compete">
    <p>
      A closed list, put here before you find it yourselves. No smarter phrasing gets us out of
      any of it.
    </p>
    <ul class="cannot">
      {cannot.map((c) => (
        <li>
          <Finding kind="contro" lang={lang} />
          <h3>{c.t}</h3>
          <p>{c.d}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Instrumental measurement of stickiness on calf tallow with a Texture Analyzer CT3, single-cycle compression, spherical nylon probe: Kowalska 2020, DOI 10.3390/biom10010115. The matrix is bovine and the measurement is instrumental rather than a panel judgment on an ovine matrix: both corrections strengthen the unfavourable datum." />
      <DataTag kind="fonte" label="Repair benchmark: physiological lipid systems in correct ratio assessed against fluticasone in a randomised trial on 121 patients. Research track 3 v4, section 5.5 and record sheet R04." />
    </p>

    <Callout tone="warn" title="The claim the category uses most, and why we do not">
      <p>
        "Tallow is just like human sebum" is true of the triglyceride fraction and nothing else.
        Human sebum runs roughly 25% wax esters and 12 to 15% squalene, neither of which tallow
        has, and its characteristic fatty acid is found in no other species.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is not a line to fix. It is a line not to use. It appears nowhere else on this site,
        and you will find it in the <a href={r.audit}>claim table</a> among the ones that hold up
        only partly.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </Section>

  <Section n="6" title="What follows">
    <p>
      Add up the five sections above. There is a sound chemical principle. There is a cheap,
      measurable parameter that governs it. There is a real complication that limits how long it
      holds. And there is no data whatsoever on what our material does to skin.
    </p>
    <p>
      The conclusion is not that tallow works. It is that <strong>nobody knows whether it works,
      and nobody is measuring what is in it</strong>. In that situation the only defensible thing
      to do is measure, and the only thing you can sell without lying is the document that
      reports the measurement.
    </p>
    <div class="closing">
      <p>The tallow is the carrier. The document is the product.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      What that document contains, field by field, is described in <a href={r.amsa}>AMSA</a>.
      Where the raw material comes from, and where its traceability breaks, is in
      <a href={r.filiera}>Supply chain</a>. What a formulator can tell their own customer without
      exposure is in <a href={r.normativa}>Regulation</a>.
    </p>
  </Section>

